<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hachisuka Iemasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1558–1639</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hachisuka Iemasa was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>'s retainer Hachisuka Masakatsu. After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> in 1585 Hideyoshi gave him Awa province, where he built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima">Tokushima</a> Castle. His son Yoshishige sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1600, securing the family's rule of the Tokushima domain until 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hachisuka-iemasa.jpg" alt="Hachisuka Iemasa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Fg2 assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokushima_Hachisuka_Iemasa_M3752b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
